// NIST ACVP known-answer vectors for ML-DSA signature verification (FIPS 204).
//
// Source: github.com/usnistgov/ACVP-Server, gen-val/json-files/ML-DSA-sigVer-FIPS204/
// (internalProjection.json, which additionally labels each case with the reason it is expected
// to be accepted or rejected). Fetched 2026-10-03 from master.
//
// Split out from kat_mldsa.cpp because it tests the opposite claim. The signing vectors there
// establish that a correct input produces the right bytes; these establish that a *wrong* input
// is refused, which is the half an implementation passes by accident. 144 of ACVP's 180 sigVer
// cases are negative, split evenly across four reasons, and each one lands on a different
// rejection path:
//
//  - **modified message** -- mu changes, so the recomputed commitment cannot match. This is the
//    only one a naive implementation gets right for free.
//  - **modified signature - commitment** -- c-tilde is altered, so SampleInBall yields a
//    different challenge and the final comparison fails.
//  - **modified signature - hint** -- normally caught by HintBitUnpack's three rejection
//    conditions (a non-monotone cumulative index, positions not strictly increasing *within one
//    polynomial*, or a non-zero byte in the unused tail) before any arithmetic runs. An
//    implementation enforcing fewer of them accepts several encodings of the same hint, which
//    makes the signature malleable.
//  - **modified signature - z** -- fails the ||z||_inf < gamma1 - beta bound. Skipping that
//    check at the verifier is invisible to every honest signature, because an honest signer
//    already enforces it on itself.
//
// This is also where the external/internal interface split is checked on the verifying side:
// the `external` records go through MlDsaScheme::verify() with the group's own context string,
// the rest through verifyInternal().
//
// Five records per parameter set for the external interface -- one of each reason -- plus the
// valid internal case for each. The complete 180 were reproduced (verdict-for-verdict) against
// a standalone Python reference written from the specification text, and then against this
// implementation.

#define DOCTEST_CONFIG_IMPLEMENT_WITH_MAIN
#include <doctest/doctest.h>

#include <certpp.hpp>
#include "crypto/asyms/mldsascheme.hpp"

#include <string>
#include <vector>

using namespace certpp;
using namespace certpp::crypto;

